1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,200
Imagine it's 2045.

2
00:00:02,050 --> 00:00:06,050
Cancer is no longer the death sentence it
once was.

3
00:00:06,300 --> 00:00:09,500
Robots handle much of the world's physical
labor.

4
00:00:09,750 --> 00:00:12,550
AI has dramatically sped up scientific
discovery.

5
00:00:13,400 --> 00:00:14,600
Energy is cheaper.

6
00:00:14,850 --> 00:00:16,450
Manufacturing is more efficient.

7
00:00:16,700 --> 00:00:19,500
And people are living longer, healthier
lives.

8
00:00:20,350 --> 00:00:24,350
Along the way, the stock market has
created enormous fortunes.

9
00:00:25,200 --> 00:00:27,200
But here's the strange part.

10
00:00:27,450 --> 00:00:31,450
Some investors who correctly predicted
this future still lost money.

11
00:00:31,700 --> 00:00:36,900
Others got rich investing in companies
nobody was talking about when the
revolution

12
00:00:36,900 --> 00:00:38,100
began.

13
00:00:38,950 --> 00:00:40,150
Why?

14
00:00:40,400 --> 00:00:44,000
Because the stock market doesn't simply
reward good news.

15
00:00:44,250 --> 00:00:48,650
It rewards outcomes that are better than
what investors already expected.

16
00:00:49,500 --> 00:00:51,500
Quick note before we start.

17
00:00:51,750 --> 00:00:54,150
This is a fictional, optimistic scenario.

18
00:00:54,400 --> 00:00:58,800
It is not a forecast, and nothing here is
investment advice.

19
00:01:01,150 --> 00:01:02,350
Chapter one.

20
00:01:02,600 --> 00:01:04,200
The infrastructure gold rush.

21
00:01:04,450 --> 00:01:05,650
2026 to 2028.

22
00:01:06,500 --> 00:01:07,700
It's late 2026.

23
00:01:07,950 --> 00:01:09,550
AI models keep improving.

24
00:01:09,800 --> 00:01:11,800
Businesses race to automate work.

25
00:01:12,050 --> 00:01:15,650
And every major tech company wants more
computing power.

26
00:01:16,500 --> 00:01:20,500
Investors focus on the obvious names, like
Nvidia and Microsoft.

27
00:01:20,750 --> 00:01:23,550
But the physical world can't keep up.

28
00:01:24,400 --> 00:01:27,000
Data centers need electricity,
transformers, cooling systems, optical
fiber,

29
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:29,600
and power grid upgrades.

30
00:01:30,450 --> 00:01:34,850
Then a relatively obscure electrical
equipment maker announces that its order

31
00:01:34,850 --> 00:01:36,050
backlog has doubled.

32
00:01:36,900 --> 00:01:38,500
Big institutional investors notice.

33
00:01:38,750 --> 00:01:43,950
Money starts rotating out of the obvious
AI winners and into the companies

34
00:01:43,950 --> 00:01:45,550
supplying the AI economy.

35
00:01:46,400 --> 00:01:47,600
The thinking shifts.

36
00:01:47,850 --> 00:01:54,250
First it was: AI is the future, so I need
to own the companies making AI.

37
00:01:55,100 --> 00:01:59,100
Then it becomes: every AI company needs
electricity and infrastructure.

38
00:01:59,350 --> 00:02:00,950
So who supplies those?

39
00:02:01,800 --> 00:02:05,400
Companies worth researching here include
Eaton, for electrical infrastructure.

40
00:02:05,650 --> 00:02:08,450
Vertiv, for data center power and cooling.

41
00:02:08,700 --> 00:02:10,300
Corning, for optical fiber.

42
00:02:10,550 --> 00:02:14,150
And GE Vernova, for power generation and
grid equipment.

43
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:16,200
The lesson.

44
00:02:16,450 --> 00:02:20,450
The early opportunity may be finding the
bottlenecks AI creates,

45
00:02:20,450 --> 00:02:22,850
before everyone else prices them in.

46
00:02:25,200 --> 00:02:26,400
Chapter two.

47
00:02:26,650 --> 00:02:28,650
The first AI biotech breakthrough.

48
00:02:28,900 --> 00:02:30,100
2028 to 2030.

49
00:02:30,950 --> 00:02:32,150
It's 2029.

50
00:02:32,400 --> 00:02:36,800
A biotech company announces remarkable
late-stage trial results for an AI-
assisted

51
00:02:36,800 --> 00:02:37,000
cancer treatment.

52
00:02:38,850 --> 00:02:42,450
The stock jumps 70 percent in a single
day.

53
00:02:42,700 --> 00:02:43,900
Financial news celebrates.

54
00:02:44,150 --> 00:02:46,550
Investors pile into AI drug discovery.

55
00:02:47,400 --> 00:02:50,200
Six months later, another company's trial
fails.

56
00:02:50,450 --> 00:02:54,050
Several speculative biotech stocks
collapse 40 to 60 percent.

57
00:02:54,900 --> 00:02:56,900
The science keeps moving forward.

58
00:02:57,150 --> 00:03:01,150
But the market swings from euphoria, to
disappointment, to consolidation.

59
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:05,600
A revolutionary industry is not
necessarily a profitable investment.

60
00:03:05,850 --> 00:03:08,250
Investors tend to overestimate short-term
progress,

61
00:03:08,250 --> 00:03:09,850
and underestimate long-term change.

62
00:03:10,700 --> 00:03:13,500
Companies worth researching include Thermo
Fisher Scientific,

63
00:03:13,500 --> 00:03:15,900
for lab equipment and research services.

64
00:03:16,150 --> 00:03:18,150
Danaher, for life science tools.

65
00:03:18,400 --> 00:03:19,000
Illumina, for DNA sequencing.

66
00:03:20,250 --> 00:03:24,650
And on the speculative end, Recursion
Pharmaceuticals, for AI-driven drug
discovery.

67
00:03:25,500 --> 00:03:26,700
The lesson.

68
00:03:26,950 --> 00:03:30,950
Companies that supply research
infrastructure can benefit from more
experimentation,

69
00:03:30,950 --> 00:03:32,950
even when individual drugs fail.

70
00:03:33,200 --> 00:03:35,600
But they can still be overpriced.

71
00:03:37,950 --> 00:03:39,150
Chapter three.

72
00:03:39,400 --> 00:03:40,000
Robots leave the factory.

73
00:03:41,250 --> 00:03:42,450
2030 to 2033.

74
00:03:43,300 --> 00:03:44,500
It's 2031.

75
00:03:44,750 --> 00:03:49,150
Humanoid robots become commercially
practical for a wide range of repetitive

76
00:03:49,150 --> 00:03:50,750
factory and logistics work.

77
00:03:51,600 --> 00:03:54,400
An early adopter announces major cost
savings.

78
00:03:54,650 --> 00:03:58,250
Competitors now face a choice: automate,
or fall behind.

79
00:03:59,100 --> 00:04:00,300
Robot orders surge.

80
00:04:01,150 --> 00:04:03,150
Investors chase the robot makers.

81
00:04:03,400 --> 00:04:04,000
But competition heats up.

82
00:04:05,250 --> 00:04:06,450
Robot prices fall.

83
00:04:06,700 --> 00:04:10,300
And some manufacturers struggle to protect
their profit margins.

84
00:04:11,150 --> 00:04:15,150
Meanwhile, the component suppliers, and
the businesses using robots well,

85
00:04:15,150 --> 00:04:17,150
end up with stronger economics.

86
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:22,800
The company that invents a revolutionary
technology doesn't always capture most of

87
00:04:22,800 --> 00:04:23,000
its value.

88
00:04:24,850 --> 00:04:28,450
Companies worth researching include
Rockwell Automation, for industrial
automation.

89
00:04:28,700 --> 00:04:30,700
ABB, for robotics and electrification.

90
00:04:30,950 --> 00:04:32,950
Teradyne, for automation and testing.

91
00:04:33,200 --> 00:04:35,200
And Cognex, for machine vision.

92
00:04:36,050 --> 00:04:37,250
The lesson.

93
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:41,500
Follow the businesses that supply, deploy,
and profit from automation.

94
00:04:41,750 --> 00:04:44,550
Not just the ones building the robots.

95
00:04:45,400 --> 00:04:47,800
And the social side is complicated.

96
00:04:48,050 --> 00:04:50,850
Productivity grows, but some workers are
displaced.

97
00:04:51,100 --> 00:04:53,900
Governments debate retraining, income
support, and taxes.

98
00:04:56,250 --> 00:04:57,450
Chapter four.

99
00:04:57,700 --> 00:04:59,700
The great AI market crash.

100
00:04:59,950 --> 00:05:01,150
2033 to 2035.

101
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:03,200
It's 2033.

102
00:05:03,450 --> 00:05:05,450
AI is better than ever.

103
00:05:05,700 --> 00:05:06,900
Robotics is expanding.

104
00:05:07,150 --> 00:05:08,350
Biotech is advancing.

105
00:05:09,200 --> 00:05:13,200
And yet, in our scenario, the Nasdaq
crashes 45 percent.

106
00:05:14,050 --> 00:05:15,250
Why?

107
00:05:16,100 --> 00:05:18,900
Because investors had priced in near-
perfect outcomes.

108
00:05:19,750 --> 00:05:22,150
Companies had spent billions on
infrastructure,

109
00:05:22,150 --> 00:05:24,950
and some of it delivered disappointing
returns.

110
00:05:25,200 --> 00:05:27,000
Competition pushed down prices for AI
services.

111
00:05:28,250 --> 00:05:29,850
Heavily indebted businesses struggled.

112
00:05:30,700 --> 00:05:35,100
Even successful companies couldn't grow
fast enough to justify their valuations.

113
00:05:35,950 --> 00:05:37,550
The technology wasn't failing.

114
00:05:37,800 --> 00:05:39,000
Expectations were.

115
00:05:39,850 --> 00:05:42,650
Picture a hypothetical AI growth stock
index.

116
00:05:42,900 --> 00:05:49,700
It starts at 100 in 2026, climbs to 420 by
2033, then falls to 231 in 2034,

117
00:05:49,700 --> 00:05:50,900
before eventually recovering.

118
00:05:51,750 --> 00:05:56,950
A company can perform well as a business,
while its stock performs terribly.

119
00:05:57,800 --> 00:06:01,800
Investors who buy at euphoric prices can
suffer huge losses.

120
00:06:02,050 --> 00:06:06,450
Investors who carefully study the
fundamentals during pessimistic periods
may find

121
00:06:06,450 --> 00:06:07,650
much better opportunities.

122
00:06:08,500 --> 00:06:09,700
The lesson.

123
00:06:09,950 --> 00:06:12,350
Predicting the future correctly isn't
enough.

124
00:06:12,600 --> 00:06:15,000
The price you pay matters enormously.

125
00:06:17,350 --> 00:06:18,550
Chapter five.

126
00:06:18,800 --> 00:06:20,400
Cancer becomes increasingly treatable.

127
00:06:20,650 --> 00:06:21,850
2035 to 2038.

128
00:06:22,700 --> 00:06:23,900
It's 2036.

129
00:06:24,150 --> 00:06:26,150
AI-designed treatments, immunotherapies,
early detection,

130
00:06:26,150 --> 00:06:29,750
and gene technologies dramatically improve
outcomes for many cancers.

131
00:06:30,600 --> 00:06:33,400
Millions of people live longer, healthier
lives.

132
00:06:34,250 --> 00:06:37,450
At first, investors focus on the drug
companies.

133
00:06:38,300 --> 00:06:40,700
Then analysts start asking bigger
questions.

134
00:06:41,550 --> 00:06:45,550
What happens when people live 10 to 15
years longer?

135
00:06:45,800 --> 00:06:49,000
What happens when older adults stay
economically active?

136
00:06:49,250 --> 00:06:52,850
What happens to retirement, travel,
insurance, and consumer spending?

137
00:06:53,700 --> 00:06:56,500
Possible winners include diagnostics and
medical imaging,

138
00:06:56,500 --> 00:07:00,900
retirement and wealth management, travel
and leisure, fitness and healthy aging,

139
00:07:00,900 --> 00:07:03,300
and consumer businesses serving older
people.

140
00:07:04,150 --> 00:07:07,350
Possible losers include certain chronic
disease treatment models,

141
00:07:07,350 --> 00:07:09,750
some insurers facing unexpected longevity
costs,

142
00:07:09,750 --> 00:07:13,350
and companies tied to medical technologies
that become obsolete.

143
00:07:14,200 --> 00:07:15,400
The lesson.

144
00:07:15,650 --> 00:07:17,250
Think beyond the breakthrough.

145
00:07:17,500 --> 00:07:21,100
Figure out what happens when the
breakthrough becomes normal.

146
00:07:23,450 --> 00:07:24,650
Chapter six.

147
00:07:24,900 --> 00:07:26,500
The age of abundance.

148
00:07:26,750 --> 00:07:27,950
2038 to 2042.

149
00:07:28,800 --> 00:07:30,000
It's 2040.

150
00:07:30,250 --> 00:07:32,250
Robots build buildings more cheaply.

151
00:07:32,500 --> 00:07:34,100
AI improves industrial processes.

152
00:07:34,350 --> 00:07:35,950
Automated farming increases output.

153
00:07:36,200 --> 00:07:39,400
And some forms of energy get much cheaper.

154
00:07:40,250 --> 00:07:41,450
Productivity surges.

155
00:07:42,300 --> 00:07:47,500
But think about an automated manufacturer
that cuts its production costs by 60

156
00:07:47,500 --> 00:07:48,700
percent.

157
00:07:49,550 --> 00:07:52,350
If its competitors make the same
improvements,

158
00:07:52,350 --> 00:07:55,150
competition may force everyone to lower
prices.

159
00:07:56,000 --> 00:07:59,600
Consumers capture much of the value,
instead of shareholders.

160
00:08:00,450 --> 00:08:04,850
Productivity growth does not automatically
turn into corporate profits or rising

161
00:08:04,850 --> 00:08:06,050
stock prices.

162
00:08:06,900 --> 00:08:08,500
So what stays scarce?

163
00:08:08,750 --> 00:08:10,350
Desirable land and locations.

164
00:08:10,600 --> 00:08:11,800
Reliable energy infrastructure.

165
00:08:12,050 --> 00:08:13,650
Trusted brands and distribution.

166
00:08:13,900 --> 00:08:15,100
Proprietary intellectual property.

167
00:08:15,350 --> 00:08:16,950
And unique customer relationships.

168
00:08:17,800 --> 00:08:19,000
The lesson.

169
00:08:19,250 --> 00:08:24,050
Look for the companies that keep pricing
power when technology makes production

170
00:08:24,050 --> 00:08:25,250
cheaper.

171
00:08:26,100 --> 00:08:28,500
Abundance may also be shared unequally.

172
00:08:28,750 --> 00:08:30,350
Ownership, regulation, housing shortages,

173
00:08:30,350 --> 00:08:34,350
and job displacement can keep progress
from reaching everyone equally.

174
00:08:36,700 --> 00:08:37,900
Chapter seven.

175
00:08:38,150 --> 00:08:39,750
The market in 2045.

176
00:08:40,600 --> 00:08:44,200
AI has become an ordinary part of economic
life.

177
00:08:44,450 --> 00:08:45,650
Robots are everywhere.

178
00:08:45,900 --> 00:08:47,500
Biotech has transformed medicine.

179
00:08:48,350 --> 00:08:52,750
Some of the celebrated AI companies of
2026 have disappeared, merged,

180
00:08:52,750 --> 00:08:54,750
or turned into mediocre investments.

181
00:08:55,600 --> 00:08:58,400
Others have grown into enormous,
profitable enterprises.

182
00:08:59,250 --> 00:09:04,050
And several of the biggest long-term
winners were companies that barely
marketed

183
00:09:04,050 --> 00:09:06,450
themselves as AI businesses at all.

184
00:09:07,300 --> 00:09:11,700
They simply supplied the things the new
economy couldn't function without.

185
00:09:12,550 --> 00:09:13,750
The lesson.

186
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:17,600
The best investors didn't necessarily
predict the most inventions.

187
00:09:17,850 --> 00:09:23,450
They understood where profits would pile
up, how much growth was already priced in,

188
00:09:23,450 --> 00:09:25,450
and when expectations got unreasonable.

189
00:09:27,800 --> 00:09:31,000
So let's turn this story into a playbook.

190
00:09:31,250 --> 00:09:32,450
Six ideas.

191
00:09:34,800 --> 00:09:36,000
Idea one.

192
00:09:36,250 --> 00:09:38,250
Follow the chain of consequences.

193
00:09:39,100 --> 00:09:41,100
Say AI helps cure cancer.

194
00:09:41,350 --> 00:09:44,950
Stage one is compute: chips, electricity,
and data centers.

195
00:09:45,200 --> 00:09:48,800
Stage two is scientific tools: sequencing
and lab automation.

196
00:09:49,050 --> 00:09:52,650
Stage three is clinical trials, drug
developers, and diagnostics.

197
00:09:52,900 --> 00:09:56,500
Stage four is longevity: insurance,
retirement, and consumer spending.

198
00:09:57,350 --> 00:10:01,750
Each stage may offer opportunities before
the next one becomes obvious.

199
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:06,400
But the timeline is uncertain, and some
breakthroughs may never arrive.

200
00:10:08,750 --> 00:10:09,950
Idea two.

201
00:10:10,200 --> 00:10:14,600
A good company is not the same as a good
stock.

202
00:10:15,450 --> 00:10:16,650
Imagine two companies.

203
00:10:16,900 --> 00:10:20,900
Both grow earnings 25 percent a year for
five years.

204
00:10:21,750 --> 00:10:26,150
Company A starts at a price to earnings
ratio of 80.

205
00:10:26,400 --> 00:10:30,000
Company B starts at a P E of 20.

206
00:10:30,850 --> 00:10:35,250
Five years later, both trade at a P E of
25.

207
00:10:36,100 --> 00:10:40,900
Ignoring dividends and dilution, Company
A's stock is down about 5 percent.

208
00:10:41,150 --> 00:10:44,350
Company B's stock is up about 280 percent.

209
00:10:45,200 --> 00:10:46,400
Same business results.

210
00:10:46,650 --> 00:10:47,850
Completely different outcomes.

211
00:10:48,100 --> 00:10:52,500
The only difference was the price
investors paid at the start.

212
00:10:54,850 --> 00:10:56,050
Idea three.

213
00:10:56,300 --> 00:10:58,300
Build an early signal watchlist.

214
00:10:59,150 --> 00:11:03,150
For AI infrastructure, research names like
Eaton, Vertiv, and Corning.

215
00:11:03,400 --> 00:11:07,000
Watch power demand, order backlogs,
construction, and grid investment.

216
00:11:07,850 --> 00:11:11,850
For AI biotech, look at Recursion, Thermo
Fisher, and Danaher.

217
00:11:12,100 --> 00:11:15,300
Watch clinical results, research budgets,
and lab automation.

218
00:11:16,150 --> 00:11:18,550
For robotics, Teradyne, ABB, and Rockwell.

219
00:11:18,800 --> 00:11:22,000
Watch deployments, falling automation
costs, and customer returns.

220
00:11:22,850 --> 00:11:25,650
For precision medicine, Illumina and
Intuitive Surgical.

221
00:11:25,900 --> 00:11:28,300
Watch clinical adoption and procedure
growth.

222
00:11:29,150 --> 00:11:31,950
For energy, GE Vernova and Constellation
Energy.

223
00:11:32,200 --> 00:11:35,400
Watch power contracts, new capacity, and
infrastructure spending.

224
00:11:36,250 --> 00:11:39,450
And for the longevity economy, Visa and
BlackRock.

225
00:11:39,700 --> 00:11:42,500
Watch demographics, spending, savings, and
retirement trends.

226
00:11:43,350 --> 00:11:46,550
These are companies to research, not
automatic buys.

227
00:11:46,800 --> 00:11:50,000
Valuation, competition, balance sheets,
and execution still matter.

228
00:11:52,350 --> 00:11:53,550
Idea four.

229
00:11:53,800 --> 00:11:55,800
Learn the psychology of expectations.

230
00:11:56,650 --> 00:11:59,450
Discovery: investors start noticing a new
opportunity.

231
00:11:59,700 --> 00:12:01,700
Optimism: early wins attract money.

232
00:12:01,950 --> 00:12:04,750
Euphoria: people assume extraordinary
outcomes are guaranteed.

233
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:07,000
Disappointment: growth misses inflated
expectations.

234
00:12:07,250 --> 00:12:09,250
Despair: investors abandon the theme.

235
00:12:09,500 --> 00:12:13,500
And adoption: real-world use keeps
growing, and lasting businesses emerge.

236
00:12:14,350 --> 00:12:17,950
It's a way of thinking, not a guaranteed
sequence.

237
00:12:20,300 --> 00:12:21,500
Idea five.

238
00:12:21,750 --> 00:12:23,350
Ask the second-order question.

239
00:12:24,200 --> 00:12:29,400
Instead of asking, which company will
invent the next big thing, ask this.

240
00:12:30,250 --> 00:12:33,450
If this technology becomes 100 times more
useful,

241
00:12:33,450 --> 00:12:37,450
what will the world suddenly need 10 times
more of?

242
00:12:37,700 --> 00:12:40,100
And who can supply it profitably?

243
00:12:40,950 --> 00:12:46,550
Then ask: how much of that future demand
is already in the stock price?

244
00:12:48,900 --> 00:12:50,100
Idea six.

245
00:12:50,350 --> 00:12:51,950
Look for three signals.

246
00:12:52,800 --> 00:12:54,000
Bottlenecks.

247
00:12:54,250 --> 00:12:57,450
Which essential resources or services are
becoming scarce?

248
00:12:58,300 --> 00:12:59,500
Adoption inflection points.

249
00:12:59,750 --> 00:13:03,750
When does a technology go from
experimental to economically practical?

250
00:13:04,600 --> 00:13:05,800
And valuation extremes.

251
00:13:06,050 --> 00:13:09,650
When are investors assuming unrealistic
success, or excessive failure?

252
00:13:12,000 --> 00:13:13,600
Here's the final thought.

253
00:13:14,450 --> 00:13:19,250
The most important investing skill may not
be predicting the next breakthrough.

254
00:13:19,500 --> 00:13:23,500
It may be understanding how breakthroughs
change demand, costs, competition,

255
00:13:23,500 --> 00:13:25,100
profits, and human expectations.

256
00:13:25,950 --> 00:13:29,950
A technology can transform civilization
while its investors lose money.

257
00:13:30,800 --> 00:13:36,400
And an ordinary-looking supplier can
